in the aftermath of yet another air  attack firefighters attempt to douse a  blaze in yemen's capital sanaa  this after a flurry of strikes on  targets in tsuna as well as on the port  city of hodeidah which killed several  people  some residents claimed that united  nations workers in a nearby building had  prior knowledge and left the area before  the attack took place  it's strange that their staff left a few  hours ahead of the airstrike which means  that they were probably notified the  saudi-led airstrikes came a day after a  series of drone and missile attacks by  houthi rebels on jeddah and other saudi  cities all refineries were among the  targets  there were no deaths reported in the  strikes but it was the most concerted  air operation by the houthis in the  seven year conflict  and in the wake of the retaliatory  attacks by the coalition the houthi  rebels announced a unilateral three-day  ceasefire expressing the hope that it  could be extended  we unilaterally announce the suspension  of missile and drone strikes and all  military actions towards the kingdom of  saudi arabia by land sea and air for a  period of three days  we affirm our readiness to turn this  declaration into a final  firm and permanent commitment if the  kingdom of saudi arabia announces and  commits itself to ending the siege and  stopping its air raids on the territory  of the republic of yemen once and for  all  earlier in the day residents of sonar  defiantly rallied in the streets of the  capital marking seven years of a  conflict in which more than 150 000  people have been killed and one that's  created one of the world's worst  humanitarian crises  alex catopoulos al jazeera
 

